  "title": "we were browsing",
  "subtext": "a series of experiments in creating art from daily internet actions",
  "descriptionMd": "Increasingly new generations [feel more themselves online than offline](https://newconsumer.com/2024/09/consumer-trends-highlights-digital-natives-tiktok-china-food-as-medicine/). More and more of the time we spend expressing ourselves, encountering new ideas and people, and discovering who we are is happening in digital space, detached from our bodies. Simultaneously, surveillance is embedded into the networks that we use to communicate and institutions are building detailed profiles of who we are through our browsing habits. \n&nbsp;\n*we were browsing* turns these daily actions and datapoints into portraits of our Internet selves. A [custom browser extension](https://spencer.place/creation/we-were-online/) consensually tracks every action taken and transforms them into live studies of common browsing actions. Swipe, scrolls, clicks and drags become cursors painting lines, windows dancing across the screen, letters and phrases filling a page, and touches rippling outwards.\n&nbsp;\nTransforming the pervasive yet invisible practice of surveillance into explicit paintings, *we were browsing* reclaims collective control of our data. Investigating how everyday traces represent who we are and what we believe, it offers these gestures as material for intimate connection and knowing.\n&nbsp;\n**Studies**\n&nbsp;\n- we were browsing\n- we were typing\n- we were scrolling\n- we were surfing\n- we were clicking",
